      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Subtle, non-radio frequency signals relying on atmospheric changes would likely exploit fluctuations in air density or temperature gradients, which can slightly alter sound wave propagation speed or attenuation—a phenomenon related to acoustic ducting. Intense, rapid pressure changes can indeed modulate air density enough to affect low-frequency sound waves, potentially creating specific acoustic pathways or distortions. However, for this to be a reliable, encoded data carrier, the method would require extreme precision, far beyond everyday atmospheric noise.",
